Looking at the code:

```javascript
let parsed = mri(argv, mriOptions)
parsed = Object.keys(parsed).reduce(
  (res, name) => {
    return {
      ...res,
      [camelcaseOptionName(name)]: parsed[name],
    }
  },
  { _: [] }
)

const args = parsed._

const options: { [k: string]: any } = {
  '--': argsAfterDoubleDashes,
}

// Set option default value
const ignoreDefault =
  command && command.config.ignoreOptionDefaultValue
    ? command.config.ignoreOptionDefaultValue
    : this.globalCommand.config.ignoreOptionDefaultValue

let transforms = Object.create(null)

for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    for (const name of cliOption.names) {
      options[name] = cliOption.config.default
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

The fix should be to check if any of the option's aliases is in the parsed result. If so, we should NOT set the default.

```javascript
for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    // Skip setting defaults if any alias was parsed
    const hasParsed = cliOption.names.some(name => name in parsed)
    if (!hasParsed) {
      for (const name of cliOption.names) {
        options[name] = cliOption.config.default
      }
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

Wait, let me think about this more carefully. The `cliOption.names` are already camelCased, and the `parsed` keys are also camelCased (after the reduce). So `name in parsed` should work correctly.

Let me trace through:
1. `cli.option("-b, --base-url <baseUrl>", ...)` -> `cliOption.names = ['b', 'baseUrl']` (after sort, shortest first)
2. mri parses `--base-url https://gitlab.com` -> returns `{_: [], baseUrl: 'https://gitlab.com', b: 'https://gitlab.com'}` (mri's alias handling)
3. After camelCase reduce: `{_: [], baseUrl: 'https://gitlab.com', b: 'https://gitlab.com'}` (already camelCased)
4. With my fix: `hasParsed = ['b', 'baseUrl'].some(name => name in parsed)` -> true (both b and baseUrl are in parsed)
5. Skip setting defaults
6. The next loop sets `options.baseUrl = 'https://gitlab.com'` and `options.b = 'https://gitlab.com'` via setDotProp
7. Result: `options.baseUrl = 'https://gitlab.com'`, `options.b = 'https://gitlab.com'`

That's the desired behavior.

For the case where nothing is parsed:
1. mri parses `[]` -> returns `{_: []}`
2. After camelCase reduce: `{_: []}`
3. With my fix: `hasParsed = ['b', 'baseUrl'].some(name => name in parsed)` -> false
4. Set defaults for both: `options.b = default`, `options.baseUrl = default`
5. Result: Both have default

This satisfies both test cases.

